00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:03.420
So what is a Standardised Operating Procedure?

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.980
A Standardised Operating Procedure, or SOP, is
a documented set of instructions that outlines

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.740
how to complete a specific task or process.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:21.130
SOP are commonly used in various industries,
including manufacturing, healthcare, and

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:26.380
food service, to ensure that tasks are
completed consistently and efficiently.

00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:32.680
A SOP typically includes a clear and
concise description of the task or

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:39.849
process, a list of necessary materials and
equipment, step by step instructions, safety

00:00:39.849 --> 00:00:42.620
precautions, and quality control measures.

00:00:43.165 --> 00:00:47.934
These instructions provide employees with
a detailed roadmap of how to complete a

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:53.244
task or process, reducing the likelihood
of errors and increasing efficiency.

00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:58.965
Let's take a look at an example
SOP and what it should include.

00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:03.515
In this example, we will look at a
SOP for a busy restaurant kitchen.

00:01:04.284 --> 00:01:10.185
So this is the SOP for the kitchen at Blue's
Restaurant, and as you can see, the first

00:01:10.185 --> 00:01:15.725
thing it has on is the SOP number, the
title, which is Blue's Restaurant Kitchen.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:20.779
And then we have who wrote the
SOP, and that was the head chef.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:26.270
It was reviewed by the kitchen manager, and
it was authorised by the restaurant manager.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.230
Each person signs the document and they also
add the date that they signed off that document.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:39.400
You also have the effective date, which is from
when the SOP is effective from, and the review

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:45.809
date, the scheduled review date, which is a year
in advance of the actual first effective date.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.050
You then also have the people who work
in the kitchen who are required to read

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.530
that and confirm that they've read it.

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:58.179
So if there are any errors or any
mishaps or anything goes wrong, you can

00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:00.490
confirm who has read it and who has not.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.169
You then come down to the
actual main body of content.

00:02:04.629 --> 00:02:10.340
This is the purpose of the SOP, which
is to maintain a clean, tidy and safe

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.089
kitchen environment in order to promote
food safety and prevent accidents.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:21.750
The scope of the SOP, this applies to all
staff members who work in the kitchen, who's

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:27.410
responsible for what, and then we come to the
actual procedures which covers personal hygiene,

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:34.079
cleaning and sanitizing surfaces, proper
storage of food and supplies, disposal of waste,

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:39.700
cleaning and maintenance of equipment, pest
control, the cleaning schedule and training.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:45.100
So everything that's included in this
procedure is there primarily to ensure the

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.450
safe and effective operation of the kitchen.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:53.410
So there's no health hazards, there's no
risks to food contamination and everybody

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.019
is aware of what they need to do.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.950
So if you came in and you were just
starting in the business, you would read

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.310
the SOP and you should fully understand
exactly what you need to do at all times.

00:03:06.075 --> 00:03:09.415
You also have the opportunity
to review or make changes.

00:03:09.984 --> 00:03:12.855
So an SOP is not necessarily set in stone.

00:03:13.424 --> 00:03:18.675
If there is an opportunity for changes,
or if changes are required due to changes

00:03:18.675 --> 00:03:23.925
in legislation or working practices, you
can then make the changes to that SOP.

00:03:24.644 --> 00:03:26.155
But they need to be recorded.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.870
So what you would do is add the new SOP
number, the effective date, what changes

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.459
were made, and the previous SOP number.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.890
So this would then change to SOP number
two, when it was effective from what

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.720
has been done to change it, and then you
would make reference to SOP number one.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.779
So this has given you a full audit trail of
the SOP from start, and how it has developed

00:03:50.779 --> 00:03:53.519
or changed to the current and existing SOP.

